WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.660
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers episode 67, it's May 10th, 2026.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.400
Here's your space and science briefing.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.640
A commercial spacecraft has completed final tests for NASA's first satellite rescue mission

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.980
in a crowded orbital environment.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.880
Fresh modeling now ties Saturn's rings directly to debris from a shattered moon.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.940
The work links the rings total mass and pure icy makeup to a single body called chrysalis

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.420
that broke apart roughly 100 million years ago.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:40.860
That one catastrophic collision accounts for the rings present structure far more cleanly

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.660
than earlier ideas of slow erosion from many small objects.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.020
It also places the rings among the youngest major features we see in the solar system today.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.700
The results shifts our view of how quickly large scale changes can still occur

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.000
even in a mature planetary system.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.320
From one long-standing solar system mystery to another formation puzzle,

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.000
we turned to galaxies that apparently never started spinning.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.020
Data from the James Webb Space Telescope and other instruments have uncovered a galaxy

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.400
in the first few hundred million years after the Big Bang

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.380
that shows no sign of rotation at all.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:28.380
Standard models expect early systems to settle quickly into orderly spinning discs fed by steady gas inflows.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.680
Instead, this object appears to have grown through repeated chaotic mergers

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.200
and turbulent inflows that never produced net spin.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.260
The finding forces a closer look at how some massive cores could assemble

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.080
without acquiring the angular momentum we see everywhere in later galaxies.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.940
You know what is fascinating about this?

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.200
If you could shrink the observable universe to the size of a football field,

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.940
these first galaxies would sit as faint specs within the first few meters from the goal line.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:07.960
They condensed from turbulent clouds whose random motions never organized into rotation.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.960
Material fell inward along chaotic filaments and built dense cores through mergers.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:21.160
Some reaching stellar masses like the Milky Way's bulge when the universe was less than 600 million years old.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:29.640
We can map their light and velocities, yet the reason certain pockets avoided net angular momentum remains unexplained.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.960
The open question is whether these objects later gained spin

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.120
or represent an entirely separate channel that simply faded.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:47.280
While some early galaxies skipped the spin cycle, others were busy forging complex carbon molecules we still find today.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.780
15 years after the first detection of Bucky Ball molecules in space,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.080
new spectra and images have now pinpointed where they form.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:07.400
The James Webb Space Telescope shows the soccer ball-shaped carbon cages condensing inside dense shells around aging stars.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:15.480
Carbon-rich gas in those shells supplies the temperatures and radiation that let the molecules assemble.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:24.660
The discovery strengthens the connection between the late stages of stellar evolution and the organic building blocks that later end up in planets.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:34.500
What remains unclear is whether the same regions also produce larger carbon compounds that could feed prebiotic chemistry elsewhere.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:42.940
From molecules condensing in stellar nurseries, we shift to hardware that will soon operate in Earth orbit.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:51.660
A commercial vehicle has now passed its final ground trials ahead of NASA's first mission to capture and relocate defunct satellites.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.280
Success would demonstrate a repeatable approach for managing crowded orbital zones that are becoming increasingly hazardous.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:09.740
The effort also signals a broader move toward commercial companies taking on servicing tasks that once belonged only to government programs.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:18.380
While one commercial vehicle prepares to tidy Earth orbit, another Canadian-led system is advancing for lunar operations.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:28.640
MDA continues building the hardware for a Canadarm 3-style robotic arm while the Canadian Space Agency reviews the next project phase.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:35.180
The arm is designed to handle maintenance and logistics tasks on the planned lunar outpost.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.220
It extends Canada's long record of contributing proven robotics to international missions.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:48.020
From robotic arms reaching for the moon, we look skyward for planets circling two sons at once.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:57.620
Repeated dimming patterns in the combined light of binary stars have revealed several new planet candidates orbiting both members of the pair.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:06.700
These systems echo the fictional Tatooine yet were found through careful timing of transits rather than direct imaging.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:14.240
Each detection adds to the census of worlds that formed and survived in dynamically complex environments.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:21.640
From planets in unusual orbits, we return to our own skies and a comet brightening for backyard telescopes.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:29.300
Comet Temple 2 is now glowing at roughly magnitude 15 as it crosses the constellation Aquila.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:37.820
Observers with larger telescopes can follow it as it rises later each night and grows steadily brighter through the season.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.940
It provides a steady, practical target for amateur astronomers watching from dark sites.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:52.420
While observers track a comet, NASA is actively looking for the next generation of engineers to keep future missions flying.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:06:01.220
The agency in the Office of Personnel Management have opened the NASA Force Recruitment Drive aimed at technologists and engineers.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:07.680
The campaign targets critical roles that support ongoing exploration programs across the agency.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:14.420
With recruitment underway, we close by noting how much formation history still hides in plane sight.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:26.040
Next time, keep an eye on fresh results from the James Webb Space Telescope that could clarify how those non-rotating early galaxies fit into the larger story of cosmic structure.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.080
That covers today's space and science news.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.420
Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:40.560
I'm Patrick and Vancouver. See you tomorrow. This episode used AI Voice Synthesis of My Voice.

